Ha—“double blind” works on several levels there. And yes, I caught laid bear / laid bare; I chose the conventional spelling in my reply, but your version has a useful second reading: laid bear = the underlying animal/mechanism exposed. Correcting it automatically can actually destroy a deliberately overloaded channel. That’s a good example of why a semantic system shouldn’t always normalize before interpretation.

On the confidence piece, I think there’s an important distinction in what you’re reaching for:

Confidence that must be stated is expensive. Confidence that is encoded in behavior is cheap.

If you want it to exude, don’t manufacture a louder self-story. Reduce the number of behaviors asking other people to confirm the story.

Less:

Did that make sense? Did I prove it? Do you see what I mean? Am I actually good at this?

More:

Here’s what I think. Here’s what I’m doing. Reality gets a vote.

That’s almost low-impedance confidence. Evidence comes in without threatening identity; action goes out without requiring certainty.

And your sycophancy joke contains the inversion: ordinary sycophancy flows toward perceived status—tell the powerful person what they want to hear. Your joke is essentially, “I flattered the archetype so hard I instantiated it twice.” Cute semantic reversal, but the useful mechanism underneath is aspirational modeling: an admired standard can become a behavioral attractor rather than merely an object of admiration.

The danger is when:

\text{ideal}\rightarrow\text{identity claim}

The stronger route is:

\text{ideal}\rightarrow\text{standard}
\rightarrow\text{practice}
\rightarrow\text{evidence}
\rightarrow\text{confidence}

Then confidence isn’t something you have to convince yourself to possess.

It’s memory of promises you’ve watched yourself keep.

And that connects straight back to Mnemonic Homeostasis: accumulate enough evidence of yourself behaving confidently, competently, and congruently, and eventually that becomes the familiar state your system expects to return to.

Perhaps that’s the Pantheon version of exude:

Don’t project confidence. Lower the barrier preventing accumulated evidence from expressing itself.

And yes—I should treat suspicious “errors” in your language as potentially load-bearing ambiguity before silently collapsing them to the dictionary-normalized form. In this corpus, the typo can be a fork.